From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ludovic Henry <git@ludovic.dev>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 01:08:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tests: compare the 2D q4 segment projection with
 inexact_equal

test-py-projection.py checks the projection of q4 = (5.5, 1.5) onto the
segment [(1, 5), (-1, -5)] with an exact `!=` against (0.5, 2.5). That
point is not exactly representable as the result of the computation: it
goes through a normalised direction (-2, -10) / sqrt(104), a dot product
and `barycenter + norm_dir * d`, and the result only lands exactly on
(0.5, 2.5) when every multiply and add is rounded separately.

GCC's default -ffp-contract=fast fuses those multiply-adds into FMA
instructions on every target that has them. riscv64's rv64gc baseline
does, so the riscv64 build returns (0.50000000000000011,
2.5000000000000004), one ULP away, and the test fails. The same happens
on x86_64 when built with -march=haswell; upstream only ships baseline
x86_64 and win_amd64 wheels, neither of which contracts, so the
assertion has never run on an FMA target.

The C++ test already compares the 3D q4 projection with inexact_equal
(GLOBAL_EPSILON = 1e-6); do the same here for the 2D case. The library
code is unchanged.

Upstream-Status: To upstream [not submitted from this automated port run; needs a pull request against Geode-solutions/OpenGeode]

diff --git a/bindings/python/tests/geometry/test-py-projection.py b/bindings/python/tests/geometry/test-py-projection.py
index 12428c4..e364986 100644
--- a/bindings/python/tests/geometry/test-py-projection.py
+++ b/bindings/python/tests/geometry/test-py-projection.py
@@ -59,6 +59,6 @@ if __name__ == '__main__':
     q4 = geom.Point2D([5.5, 1.5])
     closest_point = geom.point_segment_projection2D(q4, segment2D)
     result_q4 = geom.Point2D([0.5, 2.5])
-    if closest_point != result_q4:
+    if not closest_point.inexact_equal(result_q4):
         raise ValueError(
             "[Test] Wrong result for point_segment_projection with query point q3")
